Peter Marshall <peterm@martech.uucp> wrote:
>> [ ftp ties up off-site links and gobbles diskspace; talk/IRC tie up ]
>> [ terminals better used by students conducting online work.         ]
>
>	This has always been true, why do to academic institutions
>	suddenly decide it's a problem. ($10k buys you approx. $250k of 1984
>	space)

If you think about it, it's rather easy to see how these things can become 
a problem.  If you give users free rein (with unlimited access, high quotas 
and the like), one's system can be overwhelmed rather easily.  I'm all for 
free rein, but my systems can't afford it; that's why my disk quotas are 
somewhat low (5 Mb is the starting point), and why I don't allow games on 
most of our systems.

(Thankfully, my users have (by and large) understood the priorities; I haven't
had very many problems with personal games or clients.  I'm quite thankful for
their understanding.)

One should also consider the *overall* growth in the popularity of these
services.  I can remember a time when there were only 4 or 5 IRC servers;
do you think it was as popular then as it is today?  Toss in the growth
of anonymous FTP and the growth of the TCP/IP nets themselves, and it's
easy to see how tools once considered "on the fringe" have grown in sig-
nificance. 

--Wes

ps> As for the "$10k buys tons of space," that depends on the system.  I've
    maxxed out one of my systems; *any* disk increase will require a separate
    cabinet, additional disk controller, and additional power supply; when 
    you toss in the cost of the 2Gb disk, the price shoots over $20,000!

    Moral of the story - don't talk about the ease of expansion if you don't
    know the particulars.

