I wrote:

>By definition, if the Univeristy owns the files, then it can sell
>them. Is this really that the University means to claim?

Discussion on this point gone into intellectual property, but that was
not my intention. While a university could require students to assign
the copyright of any material created with university resources to the
university, such requirement are rare, and as far as I know, not the
case with U. of Toronto.

My point was that if the U. of Toronto "owns" all files on their
system, then they could, for example, sell off the backup tapes.
The ad could read:

     For Sale:
        A backup tape of all of Professor John Doe's files. These
        files include
           1) Discussion of tenure for Prof. Jane X.
           2) Unpublished research papers
           3) An Email archive in which Prof. Smith discusses with
              another professor the difficulties of keeping one's
              homosexuality secret.

Note that just as I don't need copyright permission to sell my
legally-obtained copy of the book _The Mac Intenet Tour Guide_, so the
U. of Toronto doesn't need copyright permission to sell a
legally-obtained backup copy of John Doe's files.

Two final points,

1) The concept of "ownership" is one of the most complex in the law.
   The phrase "the university owns the files" is, at best, so unclear
   and vague that it needs to be, at the least, clarified.

2) Any policy that contains as many unclear and vague phrases as the
   U. of Toronto (Scarborough) policy, needs to be, at least, subject
   to serious revision.

- Carl
