Several people asserted that because students don't pay the full cost
of their computer service, any university policy, no matter how bad or
arbitrary, is legitimate. In other words, beggers can't be choosers.

In the U.S., the courts have rejected this argument. They have said
that even if students don't pay the full *cost* of their education,
they do pay the full *price* and are thus consumers, not beggers. The
fact that the price is below cost, is just a business decision by the
university, and does not negate the student's rights as consumers.

Moreover, even if students in Canada are beggers with no consumer
rights, that doesn't mean that students can't complain when a small
group of computer administrators promulgate a policy that gives them
authority to act arbitrarily. Any university should act within its
rules and charter. I don't know of any university that gives its staff
authority to create a policy that gives that staff arbitrary
authority. And even if it did, student-beggers have a right, as
Canadian citizens and members of the university community, to express
their opinion of such a policy.

- Carl
