In article <2hpvon$ks7@mojo.eng.umd.edu> russotto@eng.umd.edu (Matthew T. Russotto) writes:

>happens except that they might thing you are a jerk.  Other sysadmins,
>being jerks themselves, might decide to invoke the University rule
>about failing to follow the reasonable request of a University official.

>Maybe UMass and UKy don't have such rules, but it came out last time
>that many Universities do.  I believe it is rule 9(e) at UMCP, but
>that's from memory.

UMass has that rule. But, *I'm* not a "University Official" as far as I 
know! I'm "professional staff" which sorta translates to "salaried peon."
I always thought "University Official" meant Management and the campus
cops? 

   We did get someone out of here once on a charge that amounted to 
"misuse" of  the system (don't quote me, I don't have the document 
stating the exact charge) but we gave many verbal, e-mail, and written
warnings, including having the person sign an agreement that detailed the
system rules we were asking him to follow (he was doing some *dumb* stuff, 
sharing his password repeatedly with friends, blaming them for his account
activities, arguing with us that he disagreed with the rules so he
shouldn't have to follow them...)

  Any way, I'm not sure you can make rules to protect against "fascist" 
administrators. In any system, if people are trying to railroad someone,
there's usually a way. The only solution is to have the people who care
about education make sure that the people hired are not fascists! Who's
minding the store? In these schools, how do these administrators get to
be so powerful and aren't there any people in power who care?  Who's 
in charge of the appeals process? Are *any* of the Deans interested?
 
   Maybe one lesson from all this is that people have to do some defensive
education *before* the first person gets caught in the trap. I bet there's
some professors, in the CS dept or elsewhere, who really care about
Academic Freedom at every school.... of course, some schools are so
depressing that no-one wants to fight any more battles than they have 
to. I feel sorry for the students there. They are better off elsewhere but
it's too bad that some individual reputations are shot in the process.

  One reason this place is sorta cool is that the Department was started by
people who felt their mandate was to build a good, inner-city university,
and these folks are still here. They didn't hire anyone who wasn't going to
work for that goal. We're under so much pressure from the budget cuts that
everyone ends up really caring about the job we are doing. 

