In article <CK5EGw.CHJ@cs.uiuc.edu>, Carl M Kadie <kadie@cs.uiuc.edu> wrote:
>Someone expressed the opinion that sys admins need authority to
>*arbitrarily* suspend accounts in order to handle emergencies. I don't
>think so. All that is needed is authority to suspend accounts in
>emergencies. This excerpt from the "Joint Statement on Rights and
>Freedoms of Students" explains:
>
>"C. Status of Student Pending Final Action
>
>  Pending action on the charges, the status of a student
>should not be altered, or his right to be present on the campus and to
>attend classes suspended, except for reasons relating to his physical
>or emotional safety and well being, or for reasons relating to the
>safety and well-being of students, faculty, or university property."

This citation seems to presume that due process is already underway.

I think that the original poster was referring to "on the fly" emergen-
cies, such as "this guy is running a program that is crashing the net-
work, and I've already told him not to run it."   Another possible "emer-
gency" (of a sort) case would be actually catching someone in the midst 
of a serious violation; I went through just such a situation when I found 
a user downloading (or trying to download) our entire library of PC soft-
ware from our LAN server.  

Both of these situations have occured on the systems I manage; in each 
case, I felt completely justified in terminating the sessions and locking 
the accounts (the users were greeted with a banner message instructing
them to contact me at their earliest convenience).  Both issues were re-
solved within 24 hours, and no charges were filed against the users involved.

