From caf-talk Caf Jan 24 17:25:44 1994
Subject:  INTERNET CENSORSHIP
From: steve.struthers@dt-can.com (Steve Struthers)
Message-ID: <fe.647.2006.0N0CB5A9@dt-can.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Jan 94 13:42:00 -0500

As a relative newcomer to this newsgroup and to the Internet, I am 
intrigued by the depth and intensity of opinions concerning censorship 
of opinion and reading material on the 'net.

At first blush, it would seem to me that what we are really engaged in 
here is a battle for control of the net. One one side, we have the 
camp that espouses censorship of opinion on a variety of grounds, some 
of which appear plausible on the surface, and some which are rather 
transparent pretexts, such as the alleged need, in the case of 
Scarborough College, at the University of Toronto, to conserve 
computing resources.

On the opposing side (which I support), are people who believe that 
the Internet should remain a free and open conduit for the discussion 
and exchange of ideas and materials, and that censorship of same 
serves no useful purpose, particularly where the pursuit of expanding 
our state of knowledge about the world is concerned.

Someone once said that "governments fear the exercise of freedom by 
the people." This fear, seen for what it is, appears entirely 
reasonable. After all, the overarching purpose of government, for 
better or worse, is to govern, and attempts to circumvent that 
governance is antithetical to any government's (or governing 
institution's) raison d'etre and the daily activities and duties they 
believe themselves to be charged with carrying out.

The recent explosion in Internet usage and the myriad newsgroups 
available has made the Internet a difficult thing to control. To those 
that would censor or restrict free speech, the dizzying growth of the 
Internet and the difficulty of controlling it must appear a frightening 
and daunting prospect indeed. 

Witness, then, the steps taken by several universities, notably Indiana 
U., Illinois U., Scarborough College, and now McGill University to 
either censor or restrict access to Internet newsgroups, FTP and other 
Internet services. If it is true that the chief mission of any 
university is to promote the expression of free speech and the 
acquisition of knowledge, then these recent attempts to restrict or 
control the Internet would appear to run counter to such an oft-stated 
mission. 

But since the Internet has progressed from its early days as a 
network of primarily military importance to something of enormous 
academic import, with universities now providing many of the 
Internet's resources, it would appear that the universities in North 
America, as governing institutions of a sort, are in a rather 
unique position to spearhead institutional efforts to wrest control 
of the Internet from the people it is ostensibly designed to serve. 

The recent actions of Indiana U. et al strike me as nothing other than 
a grasping and ham-handed effort to "nip things in the bud" - that is, 
bring the developments on the Internet that they view as threatening to 
a sharp, screeching halt. It seems oddly coincidental that such 
efforts by several universities appear to be taking place at 
roughly the same time. One has to wonder if these actions are isolated, 
and their congruence is merely coincidental, or if they are part of a 
larger agenda.

Perhaps the Internet has grown to the point where it has the 
potential, if not the capacity, to free much of the world from the 
yoke of institutionally promulgated censorship. This potential 
may well explain the alacrity with which the universities and others in 
North America are acting in respect to the Internet.

If they succeed, then the much-vaunted benefits of the "information 
superhighway" will be just so much puffery and empty talk.

Steve
