Steve Struthers <steve.struthers@dt-can.com> wrote:
>
>One one side, we have the camp that espouses censorship of opinion on 
>a variety of grounds, 
>
>On the opposing side (which I support), are people who believe that 
>the Internet should remain a free and open conduit for the discussion 
>and exchange of ideas and materials, 

Part of the problem lies in the fact that, for most of us, colleges and 
universities are the primary points of access to "the Net."  For some, 
this has developed into a situation where (in their mind) the university 
should provide every service they desire, and where no service should ever 
be terminated/lessened/restricted.  Obviously, this is not often possible; 
the resulting conflict is often erroneously labelled as censorship.  [ In 
recent months, we've seen such mundane things as diskspace quotas and re-
moval of the Unix "talk" program labelled as censorship. ]  In fact, it 
has reached the point at which it is almost impossible to *disprove* alle-
gations of censorship.  I was recently told, via email, that I should not 
require any justification whatsoever for increasing diskspace quotas; accor-
ding to the correspondent, I should just blindly increase quotas "until it 
becomes a problem."

Another part of the problem lies in the fact that it is still quite
difficult to separate an individual's actions from those of their
site.  I hope that you read the recent discussion of disclaimers; it
provided an interesting peek into a source of this problem.  As long
as users continue to associate individual actions with the sites from
which they were taken, we will have a great deal of difficulty in 
persuading those sites to be more liberal in their policies.  

--Wes

