> And here's to you, Vicki Robinson; Usenet loves you more than you will know.
 
 
   This is an excellent point, which I myself sometimes forget in the heat of
battle with "censors." Policies which include restrictions on disk space or
applications which use exorbitant amounts of resources are quite legitimate,
given that they reflect the limitations of a given machine or network.
 
   At the same time, there remain cases where decisions to restrict access
to certain Internet resources clearly reflect political or moral decisions.
This is censorship, since it substitutes one person's or one group's value
system for everyone else's, leaving no choice in the matter. Restrictions
which fall clearly into this category include selective bans on widely
accepted Usenet groups or pressure on users for the content of their posts
(within the same bounds that apply to other forms of public speech).
 
    These distinctions are often difficult to make; the "gray" area is
frequently large and not easily defined. It is important to make the effort,
however, especially in the case of public universities which must adhere to
very liberal standards of speech and expression. I firmly believe that
university systems administrators must learn to accept the same standards
which apply to their schools' libraries and academic departments. Only
then will the Internet begin to establish a firm, secure foundation which
guarantees the same freedoms of expression and inquiry we enjoy in the
"real world."
