In article <1994Jan21.020949.1123@alchemy.chem.utoronto.ca> mroussel@alchemy.chem.utoronto.ca (Marc Roussel) writes:

>>Ok Marc.  Let's use an analogy here.  The university claims that it owns
>>all of the files on its computers.  Now does this mean that when I park my
>>car on the university's property they own it?
>
>     That's a ridiculous analogy.  A car is real property obeying a set
>of rules set down very clearly in common and legislative law.  A file
>isn't like that.

Though I haven't followed this thread comprehensively, I think it may be
helpful to point out that claiming ownership of a file does not imply
intellectual property rights to the contents of the file.

As a simple example, I own many files containing copyrighted software.  My
ownership of those files does not imply that own the copyright to the
software contained in those files.

The university may be able to claim ownership of the files, but barring
other considerations, it's in no position to claim intellectual property
rights to the material contained within those files.  What's more, by
allowing it's users to use the files, the users probably have some implied
rights to the files they create.  (For example, the right to copy the contents
of the files.)


