I can't believe that UofT and McGill are isolated incidents. Then again,
participants at Unix Unanimous meetings in Toronto have nothing to be
surprised about, they've witnessed the politicizing of the academic
Internet take place over the last few years.

At the same time as academic access to the Internet is being curtailed
or (yech) audited, non-academic access to the Internet is booming. Whether
it's a login of your own on a commercial system or a SLIP link to a service
provider, it seems that the two major categories of users on the Internet
are going in different directions.

At the rate it's going, all the hand wringing in local user communities
about Acceptable Use Policies and political correctness (what else could
you *possibly* call a policy that won't allow you to have any "offensive"
files -- offensive to who?) on the University side of the loop may be for
naught. Organizations like ONET have the very real potential to politicize
themselves into irrelevancy.

All it will take is *one* current Onet institution's administrators to
say, "who needs this crap? It's a fraction of the price to go through
the non-academic links (because they wouldn't be subsidizing Laurentian's
link as they do within Onet) and there's less political burden."

With budget squeezes on post-secondary institutions ever on the
increase, it's only a matter of time before some university's bean
counter looks at its Onet participation, asks why, and doesn't get
an adequate answer in lieu of the commercial Internet alternatives.
Poof.

After all, universities don't build their own computers separate
from the outside world -- why do they need a separate network?
Do the taxpayers of the province owe them one?

Given policies like the one at UofT, grad students and others in the
academic community who value privacy, flexibility and the freedom to
own/make offensive files, will be forced to plug into the commercial
Internet anyway.

This is, after all, certainly not an issue of resources. Universities
could easily adopt a plan similar to the one described at UBC to allow
students to pay for their use of the resources beyond the basics. Make
no mistake, this is an issue of power and control.

Onet has already blown *BIG TIME* its opportunity to fund itself by
welcoming the non-academic community rather than make a pastime of
building non-technical political obstacles. It, and member academic
organizations with the same mindset, have the very real ability to
render themselves to Internet trivia.

