>}discussions don't see as much of a difference as I.
>
>No, that just makes it worse-- "you know, doing XXX might be a problem
>for some users" not only indicates a request on the part of the admin,
>it indicates that the user would be disturbing other university
>activities in doing so--- and there's another catchall regulation at
>many universities for that.

OK, then, here's the question: how am I, as an admin, supposed to
*advise* users of potential problems?  Am/should I be required to
preface every such comment with "This is only friendly advice, but"?
Is "you can do this most of the time, but keep an eye out for XXX"
acceptable?   Am I supposed to wait until it becomes a problem, with 
all its attendant bureaucratic/due process/wrangling baggage firmly 
in tow? Are "anything goes" and "taking disciplinary action" to be 
my only choices?

One of my obligations as a system administrator is to *avoid* prob-
lems, not just to clean up after them.  Sure, it's intangible; there's
no way around it.  It's impossible to clearly delineate every set of
rights and responsibilities in a computing environment; something that
is acceptable today might not be acceptable tomorrow, or even a few
hours from now, as the load on the systems fluctuates.  

I would argue that a good admin, given some degree of leeway, can 
actually be *more* beneficial to actual users than one whose steps 
are rigidly determined by policy.  Many participants in these dis-
cussions bemoan the inapproachability and/or inflexibility of their 
system admins.  Doesn't the "no middle ground" attitude presented 
in your reply actually *increase* such inflexibility?

--Wes

