In article <2hneb6$nd3@mojo.eng.umd.edu> russotto@eng.umd.edu (Matthew T. Russotto) writes:

>Uhh, is killing his processes and leaving him a email message telling him to
>cut it out and contact the system admininistrator within 'x' days or
>face account suspension somehow not going to work?

>If you shut down his account immediately, and he had a project due the
>next day, which you caused him to fail, you've created a headache for
>yourself for quite a few years.

The example we had was a student who was running a program that kept
other users from using their computer.

If something is bringing down the system so that other users cannot use
it, it is perfectly reasonable to kill the job. If the user persists, 
restarts the process, *and* ignores e-mail and "talk" requests (that's a
lot of if's) then it should be ok to suspend the account until you talk 
to the user.
   For all we know, if no-one else can use the system, the offending 
job might be thrashing and not even working for the user. Some
users will wait a looooooooooong time for that prompt...
   Me, if a user ignores talk and e-mail, and keeps starting something 
that is doing something awful to the system, I sometimes log them
off, *then* use "talk"  when they log back on. They usually take the
hint!!! It's a bit rude but so's a system crash.

  If a user has a project that really needs 100% of a workstation that is
allocated for shared use, that user needs to see us about getting time
on a bigger machine, or see us and the professor about tailoring the
project. It *is* against some rule for professors to assign homework which
a student cannot possibly finish using the resources available! This isn't
something a student should have to fight a professor on!
 
If something  we do causes loss of access, including system crashes and
system installation screwups as well as account suspensions, and the 
student asks us to, we will always contact the professor. We've never had a
problem with this, although one  professor says that the students should
allow for system downtime when completing a project and grades down for 
lateness, on the theory that in the real world, you don't get allowances 
for system  downtime. (most of our suspensions are because of cracked
passwords, and we don't suspend during critical times unless we see
abuse.)

 If the student was suspended overnight after some sort of *deliberate*
hacking that had been **repeated after a warning** (again, that's a lot of
if's) then I have NO sympathy. What about the other students who could not
use the workstation? If someone is *deliberately* hacking the system, they 
deserve any penalty that results from their hacking.  Life has
consequences. In the example given, the student was deliberately trying to
monopolize a resource that was supposed to be shared. Give us a different
example!

Betsy

