Matthew T. Russotto <russotto@eng.umd.edu> wrote:
>morgan@engr.uky.edu (Wes Morgan) writes:
>}Are "anything goes" and "taking disciplinary action" to be 
>}my only choices?
>
>No, "anything goes" and forcing your user to be exposed to
>disciplinary action regardless of whether or not his original
>activities were a violation of campus codes are your only choices.

Geez...I may as well just cut the words "compromise" and "advise" 
out of my dictionary.  8(

>You can make a request, and not take disciplinary action even if the
>user refuses to comply, but the user cannot count on that.  If you
>make a request, he must realize that he can be disciplined for
>refusing to comply.

I still maintain that there is a third category - advice.

When dealing with users, I can advise them, make requests of them, or
give them directions.  I consider those quite distinct categories, and
I always make that distinction quite clear.  

If I take the "anything goes" attitude, I can't really do *anything*
until a real problem occurs.  Once a real problem occurs, the bureau-
crats and control freaks have a legitimate (or nearly legitimate) rea-
son to demand disciplinary action.  If I can step in with *advice* and
*compromise* and head off the real problem, I avoid disciplinary action
*and* bureaucratic displeasure.  It's a tightrope, but it's the closest
thing to a win-win situation that can be mustered.

>}Doesn't the "no middle ground" attitude presented 
>}in your reply actually *increase* such inflexibility?
>
>Certainly.  It's too bad.  I didn't write the rules, and I don't like
>them.  I've always had the nerve to ignore a "request" from an authority
>if I wanted to-- and I've been punished for it more than once.

I know that you've been burned by bad admins/bureaucracy, but your pos-
tings have always championed user causes; if you value the "users' cause,"
you should be able to accept the notion that users and admins *can* work
together without the spectre of disciplinary action looming behind the
admins' every word.  It may not happen everywhere, but let's give it a
chance to work in each unique environment/situation, eh?

>Consider this situation:  A user complains about another user's
>sexually suggestive background screen, to the sysadmin.  The sysadmin
>"requests" that the second user not display the screen.  The first user
>refuses.  The sysadmin can now start disciplinary proceedings, but
>assume he does not.  The first user comes back and sees the second
>user's screen.  Not having gotten satisfaction the first time, the
>first user goes to the system administrator's boss, or some other
>university official who would prefer not to be bothered.  He starts
>creating heat for the sysadmin, who responds that he asked the user
>not to display the screen... now there's disciplinary action for sure.

Your scenario might hold true for some (many?) environments, but you
can't generalize to all of them.  8)  

If this happened on my systems, I wouldn't be in a position to even
*request*, much less demand, the removal of the display; our policy
doesn't address such issues.  The offended student could, of course,
file sexual harassment charges, but I would be uninvoled in that;
after all, I have to follow the policy, right?

I think that your comments (and, unfortunately, your past experience)
underline the need for communication between users and admins *before*
problems occur.  As I've said many times, it's always a good idea to
talk with your admins if you have *any* questions about the propriety
of a particular action (or course of action).  At the very least, you
can learn their perspective/tendencies without actually getting into
trouble; at best, you will impress them as a concerned user.  Although
I try to meet every user's needs (and most of their wants), I am far more 
likely to "push the envelope" for a user who is courteous enough to ask 
advice before going about their business...

--Wes

