In <CK7H8J.401@cs.uiuc.edu> kadie@cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M Kadie) writes:

>To me, it is obvious that internet access aids the academic mission
>(usually) in a cost effective way and that students without such
>access are at a competitive disadvantage relative to those that do.
>Universities have a duty to aid the academic mission when it is
>cost-effective. Those that do not, do their students a disservice.

In other words: 
	1. Free internet access is an academic benefit.
	2. Universities are obligated to provide any and every affordable
	   academic benefit to their students. 
	3. Not doing so injures these students by placing them at a 
	   competitive disadvantage.

Most of this seems to have nothing to do with internet access, per se, but 
expresses broader notions about the rights and duties of universities with 
respect to their students. 

Let me address each one.

1. Free internet access is an academic benefit.

Agreed.

2. Universities are obligated to provide any and every affordable academic
   benefit to their students.

This assertion certainly needs substantiation. Can Mr. Kadie quote any legal,
governmental, or academic document that makes this claim, or anything
close to it? Would Mr. Kadie care to name a university (any university) which
adequately fulfils this obligation?

It occurs to me that there must be a vast number of possible affordable 
academic benefits that a university could provide to its students, and it is
quite likely that no university in the world provides all of them. If this
statement is true, then in all probability, no university anywhere adequately
fulfils its obligations to its students. 

3. Not doing so injures their students by placing them at a competitive
   disadvantage.

Perhaps by "competitive disadvantage", Mr. Kadie means that students 
graduating with a degree from the university in question would be at a 
disadvantage when competing with students with a degree from some other 
university. But this is hardly an unusual occurrence. Graduates with
degrees from the little college down the block generally are at a competitive
disadvantage with respect to graduates from Harvard or Yale, but the college
down the block is not normally considered to have injured its students as
a result. If this were so, then almost every university on the globe would be 
guilty of injuring its students. 

On the other hand, if by "competitive disadvantage", Mr. Kadie means that
students graduating from a school that does not offer the desired academic
benefit do not have some characteristic required or expected of a person 
holding a university degree (thus placing that person at a disadvantage when 
competing with most or all other degree-holders), then perhaps such a thing
would indeed constitute a form of injury. It is then incumbent on Mr. Kadie
to demonstrate that the desired academic benefit (in this case, free internet
access) is necessary to produce some characteristic required or expected of
a person holding a university degree. 

Regards,

John
