sysadmins, would assert that these standards do not apply to electronic
media and storage facilities--they they are somehow different. At this
point, the conventional wisdom would support their assertion, simply
because this is such a new area for legal and constitutional precedent.
I would venture to say, however, that this is a situation which will change
very quickly, as private lawsuits and cases sponsored by groups such as
EFF begin to define to what extent First Amendment considerations bind
computer networks at public universities. I would also guess that quite
a few sysadmins who consider their word "final" on who does what on "their"
university-owned systems are in for a rude awakening as this process
continues.
 
