Please limit your posts to .ca, if you're going to persist in this
ignorance.  Canada may not be a signatory to this Bern Convention,
but the U.S. and most (all?) of western Europe is.  (If it *is*
a signatory to the Bern Convention, limit your posts to /dev/null.)

*ANYTHING* *ANYONE* does which is more than simply copying an existing
document is an original work under the Bern Convention.  Period.

*Any* program a student writes is original material, and copyrighted.

*Any* note the student takes in class is original material, and copyrighted.

The *highlighting* of the textbook by the student is original material,
and copyrighted.

The *doddles* the student draws on his notebook when bored in class
is original material, and copyrighted.

You may find a CS101 binary search algorithm boring and unoriginal,
but legally it is considered an original expression and fully copyrighted. 


I think the same person commented about companies owning patents of
employees.  Once again, totally bogus, at least in the U.S.  A company
may *request* a potential employee sign over patent interests to ideas
developed at work, but they don't own the patent interests merely because
the person is an employee.  (If the person refuses to sign the document,
the company can refuse to hire him.)

Copyright of material produced: 

 * by a bona fide employee (not independent contractor)
 * for the company
 * as part of the person's job duties
 * under the supervision/guidance of a company representative

belongs to the company as a "work of hire."  However, there have been
cases where copyright stayed with the creator since the courts held
that the person was a independent contractor, not an employee.  In
general, employment agreements cover this explicitly.

A student performing a class assignment is not an employee.  The student
pays the university money, not the other way around.  The student has
complete (or very extensive) creative control over the product, the
university can do no more than assign a lower grade if the product is
not what the professor wanted.  The student selects his own hours,
often can choose to use his own tools (e.g., his PC at home), etc.

In short, the university has absolutely no legal right to work produced
on its systems, except as contractually agreed when the terms and
limitations of system access were negotiated.

Does the university have the right to set policy for *new* accounts?
Yes, but it is a *contractual* matter.  The university does not 
intrinsically own anything produced by the students in any way, and
in fact should get contractual permission to perform backups of the
system!  It may choose to impose severe limitations on the students,
but then should also *explicitly* acknowledge that coursework may
be performed elsewhere.  E.g., if the professor wants to require
students use a program, it *must* be available on a non-University
system.  Finally, the university has the right to change the existing
policy for current accounts only as allowed under the current contract
with those users.

