>that many students go to university to prepare for.

Let's get our definitions straight before we go overboard.

1) If Carl is saying that experience *with networks* is a competetive advan-
   tage, he's absolutely correct.  Many employers prefer to hire folks who
   know how to use tools such as telnet, email and ftp; some prefer that their
   hires know their way around PC LANs, such as Novell or StarLAN, even if
   they are in non-technical positions.  These requirements/preferences are 
   often seen in job advertisements.  (It should be noted that an Internet
   connection is *not* necessary for the development of this skillset.)

2) If, on the other hand, Carl is arguing that experience *with network ser-
   vices*, such as Usenet, Gopher, Mosaic, WAIS et al. is a competetive ad-
   vantage, I'll have to disagree to some degree.  I have yet to see employers 
   actively seeking new employees skilled in the use of those tools.  I cannot
   recall seeing any job advertisements that required familiarity with any of
   these tools, and I (for one) have never been asked about their use in an
   interview.  (Since I'm a systems admin, I have been asked about *managing*
   Usenet, Gopher, et cetera; however, users never see that end of the tools
   anyway, so that would seem irrelevant in this particular discussion.)

3) If Carl is really talking about the advantage gained *from the knowledge
   gained* through the use of these facilities, that would seem far too 
   ambiguous (and, probably, inquantifiable) to discuss.   One could make a
   nearly identical argument about the presence/lack on one's campus of a 
   satellite dish, electron microscope, cable television, or subscriptions 
   to particular journals.  This argument immediately returns to the ques-
   tion of resource distribution; no college/university can afford everything.

--Wes

