Right. The whole idea of patenting is to for the applicant to provide the
government with a complete description of how to construct a device (no longer
has to be physical) which will be available for anyone who cares to get a
copy of the patent in exchange for a monopoly on COMMERCIAL use, i.e.
anything intended to make money of that device for a fixed term of years.

Patent infringment suits basically get the plaintiff if he (or it) wins a
fair royalty based on the sales of the device by the infringing party, these
are civil suits, not criminal.                                                     
                                
Brecher seems to have confused patents with copyrights (e.g. if someone posts
a copy of ViaCrypt's commercial PGP version for ftp, their distributing it
would be just as illegal as distributing Lotus 1-2-3 at their site... but
this obviously doesn't apply here.)

His grasp of the law is a trifle faulty. He needs to get advice on this from
