From caf-talk Caf Jan 28 21:20:51 1994
From: dleblanc@kub.nl (David Leblanc)
Subject:  British libel law & Godfrey
Message-ID: <1994Jan26.134449.26866@kub.nl>
Date: Wed, 26 Jan 94 13:44:49 GMT

There was a big libel case just decided in England yesterday (the
supposed libeler won) and the BBC ran a big discussion of British
libel law. What with Godfrey's threats being bandied about, I thought
I'd report on some of what was said.

First off, British libel law is screwy - all the commentators agreed
on that. It seems everyone in the world is launching their suits in
England even if they have only minor relevence there in order to take
advantage of the system.

The system is actually quite simple - the two parties go into court
and argue about who's lying. Melicious intent and damage to reputation
have nothing to do with the verdict, just the award of damages. All
cases are heard by judge and jury, with the jury deciding both the
verdict and the damages. In the case being discussed, the complaintant
lost so there were no damages. But, if the plaintiff loses they are
automatically assessed all costs of the trial (defendents legal
expenses, court time, etc.). In this case it worked out to 500,000
pounds. Nothing was said about if the defendent has to cover these
costs if they lose, but I would assume so. 

So, if Godfrey loses his cases, he has to pay for everything. What are
the chances of him winning. IMO, very poor. If it were a simple case
of somebody having published the offending accusations in a paper, it
would be clear-cut, he'd win. I doubt he'd get anything for damages
though, because of the following issues.

1) Proving extreme damage (or any damage) to his reputation would be
   difficult. After the presentation of his own postings on a
   multitude of subjects, and a number of examples of responses to 
   him, it would be hard to convince anyone that his reputation could
   be damaged in any way.
2) Godfrey had equal access to the medium, providing him with ample
   opportunity to defend himself (which he did).
3) Damage awards are partly based on sympathy with the plantiff. Does
   anyone think Godfrey would come out of a trial with any sympathy?

But this is of course based on an assumption that Godfrey could win.
Could he? I very much doubt it. He has two very large hurdles to get
over before the verdict would be up for contention. First off, he must
prove that the the offending person wrote the posting. With forgery
being rampent on the net both from manipulation of the technology and
simply typing into someone's unattended terminal, this will be
extremely difficult if not impossible to prove. Secondly, and this is
the big question, is the net a publication? This is particularly
important for suits against universities rather than individuals. The
arguments that the net is a publication are weak. There is no limit to
access and no editorial process. I don't see how a university can be
held any more accountable for a net posting than it could for somebody
typing something up on a university typewriter and then distributing
it by hand. The net more resembles a big conference call than a
publication. Even if it were deemed a publication, it's still doubtful
that a university could be held responsible for a student's postings.
They simply rent the equipment to the student (by way of tuition),
they have no control over what's done with it.

So, that's my non-lawyer assessment of the situation. Let's hope that
someone actually takes Godfrey up on his offer of court time. After
all, if by some miricle he wins, he probably won't win big - I can't
see any jury awarding him spit after his postings are paraded around
the courtroom. But if he loses, he loses big - costs so far of every
unresolved suit. Here's hoping.

David LeBlanc
