			------------------------

   UNIX was originally developed for open use by small groups of researchers
or engineers. That legacy is still with us in systems which assume a high
degree of trust and cooperation among the users. The evolution of our
cozy networks into the "Information Highway" ( sigh ...) means that 
issues of privacy and protection (and accessibility ...) assume an urgent
importance. 

After two years of cellular phone use, I would say that these are important
issues to be resolved NOW; for example, you can't enable international calls
on your cellular phone without signing away your life and soul because there
is a security hole in the system which snooping lowlifes have 
been listening for and exploiting ...

This suggests to me that an important new technology 
can be seriously compromised later if security and privacy issues are not 
addressed early in its development.

