In article <CKAsCC.3xM@Colorado.EDU> bear@cs.colorado.edu (Bear Giles) writes:
>*ANYTHING* *ANYONE* does which is more than simply copying an existing
>document is an original work under the Bern Convention.  Period.

Not true.  A very well-known precedent was set with _Feist Publications
vs. Rural Telephone Service_, a U.S. Supreme Court case.  The Court
ruled that the telephone directory (white pages) could not be protected
by copyright law because they contained no "original expression."
The phone book is, of course, much more than "copying an existing
document"; there is a lot of research and work involved in putting
together such a book.  However, as Pamela Samuelson reports ("Copyright
Law and Electronic Compilations of Data", _Communications of the ACM_,
February 1992/Vol.35,No.2, pp.27-32):

    Although repeating the oft-cited statement that the standard
    of originality in copyright law is low, the Court found the
    white pages of Rural's telephone directory to be "utterly
    lacking in originality," and "devoid of een the slightest
    trace of creativity."  It was, consequently, not protectable
    by copyright law.

(Reproduced without permission :-) )

>You may find a CS101 binary search algorithm boring and unoriginal,
>but legally it is considered an original expression and fully copyrighted. 

Be careful with your wording; algorithms are not protectable.  The
program which *implements* the algorithm is copyrighted.  (Or would be,
if not for the fact that six times out of ten the CS101 student typed
the program verbatim from the course text.)

