We know that - Canada has been a signatory to the Berne Convention for a
lot longer than the US has (ironic, it's usually Canadians telling Americans
how Berne works, because many Americans don't know about Berne yet).  And
accepts that.  But...

>I think the same person commented about companies owning patents of
>employees.  Once again, totally bogus, at least in the U.S.  A company
>may *request* a potential employee sign over patent interests to ideas
>developed at work, but they don't own the patent interests merely because
>the person is an employee.  (If the person refuses to sign the document,
>the company can refuse to hire him.)

The Berne convention applies to the workplace too, but...

As you acknowledge, a company can refuse to hire someone who refuses to
sign an IP agreement (as part of the employment contract) that assigns
patent/copyright for anything that the employee does to the employer.
In Canada, *many* (but not all) Universities do exactly the same thing,
as part of the student Rules and Regulations that the student
contractually agrees to upon enrolling, the University may require that
the student relinquish patent/copyright to the University.

The only difference between the US and Canada in this regard is that the
US has legislation prohibiting Universities from requiring
patent/copyright be relinquished to the University in their student
"contracts".  This has nothing to do with Berne, other than Berne being
the basis from which the US has additional IP legislation on top of
Berne.  Where much of that US legislation is hangovers from pre-Berne days
Ie: registration, which now only affects possible damage settlements,
rather than being the way you copyrighted a work as it used to be.
