From caf-talk Caf Jan 30 12:43:58 1994
Newsgroups: alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk,alt.censorship
From: jthomas@well.sf.ca.us (Jim Thomas)
Subject: Re: [alt.censorship]  RE: FIRED RETIRED PROFESSOR
Message-ID: <CKFJn4.CL4@well.sf.ca.us>
Date: Sun, 30 Jan 1994 06:35:28 GMT

>From: FPBS000@MUSICB.MCGILL.CA (MOUKDAD,HAIDAR ALI,MR)
>
>This professor of yours advocated in his article violence and was
>clearly out of line. It is not an issue of free speech or constitutional
>rights. He was a public figure in the university, and his classes were
>attended by many sudents of both sexes. When you are a bad influence on
>your students, you should not be teaching. The university acted properly
>when it forced him to retire, and that was not disgraceful at all. It
>would have been a disgrace to keep him as an educator.

Although I'm still unclear on all the facts in the case, it appears
that--if Yaqzan was "retired" solely on the basis of this article,
a travesty occured. One's status as a university professor does not
automatically translate into the status of a "public figure."
Nor--according to past posts in this and other forums--does publishing
an op-ed piece. I have read the Yaqzan article, and, silly and it is,
he does not advocate violence. From what I have read here and in another
forum, there is no public evidence that Yazqan advocated his views in
his math class. To administratively censure a professor (or anybody else)
for expressing views in a public forum, even if unpalatable to most of us,
is indeed disgraceful, because it violates the fundamental tenents 
of academic freedom.

Note that I am not speaking directly to the specifics of the Yazqan
incident, because it's possible that there are backstage facts to which
the public isn't privy. However, to accept, on principle, the
appropriateness of formal sanctions against expressing an unpopular
view is anathema to both (US) free speech and academic freedom. 

It's instructive to remember that in the US in the 1960s, anti-war
professors could be (and were) labelled as a "bad influence" on 
students and should not be teaching. 

Jim Thomas

